 ### EWA-based Attribute Interpolation
-An interpolation method for smooth attribute interpolation of normals and other attributes, based on Elliptical Weighted-Average (EWA) splatting [S16]. It is a post-rasterisation pass to smoothly interpolate normals and other attributes using EWA splatting, because the CGC data structure does not allow random access to neighboring grid cells [S16]. It provides smooth attribute interpolation compared to standard point-based methods, improving visual quality [S16]. Unlike Laine et al. [12] who use post-processing Poisson disk averaging (expensive, up to 96 samples, fails on specular surfaces), this method interpolates before shading and provides smoother normals without needing TAA or excessive samples [S16]. Unlike Jablonski et al. [10] who use a 3x3 Gaussian filter, this method uses EWA splatting which provides smoother normals [S16]. Unlike storing additional corner values (e.g., 32 instead of 8) which increases memory significantly, this method uses interpolation [S16].
+An interpolation method for smooth attribute interpolation of normals and other attributes, based on Elliptical Weighted-Average (EWA) splatting [S17]. It is a post-rasterisation pass to smoothly interpolate normals and other attributes using EWA splatting, because the CGC data structure does not allow random access to neighboring grid cells [S17]. It renders cells as round splats with a weighted footprint and accumulates attributes (normals) to the G-buffer [S17]. It provides smooth attribute interpolation compared to standard point-based methods, improving visual quality [S17]. Unlike Laine et al. [12] who use post-processing Poisson disk averaging (expensive, up to 96 samples, fails on specular surfaces), this method interpolates before shading and provides smoother normals without needing TAA or excessive samples [S17]. Unlike Jablonski et al. [10] who use a 3x3 Gaussian filter, this method uses EWA splatting which provides smoother normals [S17]. Unlike storing additional corner values (e.g., 32 instead of 8) which increases memory significantly, this method uses interpolation [S17].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot A)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels and $A$ is the average area of the footprint in pixels. The computation of the Jacobian and variance transformation is $O(1)$ per voxel [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store voxel parameters. The image buffer is $O(W \cdot H)$ [G].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Rendering time was approximately 11 seconds per frame on an 866 MHz Pentium-III for the UNC CT scan, engine, and foot datasets [S8, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. On a GeForce FX Ultra 5900, a 256^2 x 225 CT scan and 256^2 x 110 engine were rendered [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High-quality antialiasing without excessive blurring; handles arbitrary elliptical kernels; suitable for regular, rectilinear, and irregular volume datasets; universal splat primitive for surface and volume data [S2][S8, unofficial][S15, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive due to the evaluation of the resampling filter; assumes self-occlusion is ignored; basic splatting methods suffer from artifacts due to incorrect visibility determination caused by the assumption that reconstruction kernels do not overlap [S8, unofficial][S15, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality, aliasing-free volume rendering is required, especially for non-spherical kernels or perspective projections [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and the computational cost of the EWA filter is prohibitive; consider Adaptive EWA Splatting [S6, unofficial][S11] or simpler splatting methods [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot A)$, where $N$ is the number of splats and $A$ is the average number of pixels covered by a splat footprint. The computation of the Jacobian and variance transformation is $O(1)$ per splat [S6, S9, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat parameters, plus $O(R^2)$ for the footprint table if precomputed, where $R$ is the cutoff radius [S9, S13, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** On an SGI Indigo with 200 MHz, EWA splatting rendered the MRbrain dataset (2602 viewport) in 30 seconds [S1, unofficial]. On a 866 MHz Pentium-III, the Skull dataset rendered in 11 seconds [S1, unofficial]. Hardware acceleration can significantly improve this, but the algorithm is computationally intensive due to the per-pixel Gaussian evaluation and matrix operations [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** High image quality, aliasing-free, handles arbitrary elliptical kernels, perspective-correct [S2][S9, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive, requires careful handling of compositing order, sensitive to near-singular projections [S1, S6, S13, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** High-quality offline or interactive volume rendering where aliasing is unacceptable and hardware supports efficient matrix operations [S2][S9, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time applications with very large datasets and limited GPU compute power, where adaptive approximations or simpler splatting methods are preferred [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-EWA Splatting was introduced by Matthias Zwicker, Hanspeter Pfister, Jeroen van Baar, and Markus Gross in 2001 [S8, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. It is named after Heckbert's EWA filter for texture mapping due to the similarity in derivation [S7, abstract only, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitations of Westover's original framework, which did not deal with sampling rate changes due to perspective projections, leading to aliasing artifacts [S8, unofficial][S15, unofficial]. It was developed to provide high-quality splatting for both volume data and point-sampled surface data [S2][S7, abstract only, unofficial][S10, unofficial].
+EWA Splatting was introduced by Matthias Zwicker, Hanspeter Pfister, Jeroen van Baar, and Markus Gross in 2001 [S9, S16, unofficial]. It is named after Heckbert's Elliptical Weighted Average (EWA) filter for texture mapping, due to the similarity in derivation [S8, abstract only, unofficial][S9, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the aliasing artifacts and visual holes in point-sampled surface and volume rendering [S2, S7]. It builds on the concept of splatting [23-splatting.md] and extends it with a rigorous band-limited resampling filter [S9, unofficial].
 
